onents-are-anticomplete-and-anticomponents-complete","used_for":"The anticomponent blockade alternative.","opened":true},{"id":"lem-leaf-reducible-families-yield-a-large-anticomplete-pair-or-a-more-restricted-induced-subgraph","used_for":"The complement-sparse branch of the four-outcome reduction.","opened":true},{"id":"lem-nth-term-test","used_for":"Boundary divergence examples.","opened":true},{"id":"lem-p-adic-valuation-on-q","used_for":"The p-adic absolute value and ultrametric inequality.","opened":true},{"id":"lem-q-and-irrationals-dense-r","used_for":"The weak-approximation density formulation.","opened":true},{"id":"lem-restrictedness-is-complement-invariant","used_for":"The complement-sparse generalized-niceness branch.","opened":true},{"id":"prop-divisor-functions-under-dirichlet-convolution","used_for":"The divisor-counting zeta-square identity.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-absolute-convergence-criterion-for-complex-infinite-products","used_for":"Euler-product convergence and nonvanishing.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-alternating-series-test","used_for":"The mixed boundary-line counterexample.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-bezout-identity","used_for":"The nonarchimedean branch of Ostrowski's theorem.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-canonical-prime-factorisation","used_for":"Product formulas and p-adic valuations.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-chinese-remainder-theorem","used_for":"Weak approximation at finite places.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-complete-and-totally-bounded-implies-compact","used_for":"Compactness of Z_p in Q_p.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-euclid-infinitude-of-primes","used_for":"The zeta/Euclid agreement remark.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-euler-reflection-formula","used_for":"The classical zeta functional equation.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-fundamental-theorem-of-arithmetic","used_for":"Euler products, Ostrowski, and arithmetic functions.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-gamma-meromorphic-continuation","used_for":"Global zeta continuation and special values.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-gaussian-integral","used_for":"The theta transformation normalization.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-geometric-series","used_for":"Euler factors and boundary examples.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-hadamard-factorization-for-finite-order-entire-functions","used_for":"The xi Hadamard product.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-kernel-and-universal-property-of-adic-completion","used_for":"Identifying the adic Z_p model.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-legendre-duplication-formula","used_for":"The classical zeta functional equation.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-metric-completion-exists","used_for":"Cauchy-sequence construction of Q_p.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-mittag-leffler-expansion-of-pi-cotangent","used_for":"Positive even zeta values.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-p-series-rational","used_for":"Dirichlet and zeta convergence comparisons.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-rationals-countable","used_for":"The weak-approximation density formulation.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-stirling-formula-gamma","used_for":"The order-one estimate for xi.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-sum-of-totients-over-divisors","used_for":"The totient convolution identity.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces","used_for":"The theta-Mellin sum-integral interchange.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-totient-of-a-prime-power","used_for":"The totient local factor example.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-weierstrass-convergence-holomorphic-functions","used_for":"Holomorphy and termwise differentiation of Dirichlet series.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-weierstrass-m-test-for-complex-function-series","used_for":"Uniform convergence of zeta, eta, and Dirichlet series.","opened":true}],"concerns":[{"id":"lem-nonarchimedean-absolute-value-criterion","concern":"Step 1.2 applies a maximum bound to the binomial expansion using only the ordinary triangle inequality: it states |(x+y)^m| is at most the maximum of the binomial terms, whereas that inequality only gives their sum. Replacing the bound by m+1 and taking m-th roots repairs the argument.","severity":"gap-a-reader-closes"},{"id":"thm-equivalent-field-absolute-values-characterisation","concern":"In step 2.1, m/n < log|x|_1/log|a|_1 implies |a|_1^m < |x|_1^n, not the displayed reverse inequality. The subsequent inequality for the second absolute value must be reversed as well.","severity":"gap-a-reader-closes"},{"id":"thm-p-adic-absolute-value-is-nonarchimedean","concern":"Step 1.2 omits the cases x=0,y nonzero and y=0,x nonzero: they satisfy neither x+y=0 nor the stated condition that x,y,x+y are all nonzero.","severity":"gap-a-reader-closes"},{"id":"lem-p-adic-balls-are-clopen","concern":"The nesting assertion is false if “two balls” permits one closed and one open ball. With equal centre and radius r, the closed ball intersects but is not contained in the open ball of radius r; step 3.1 proves only the all-open and all-closed cases.","severity":"would-be-fatal"},{"id":"cor-p-adic-field-is-locally-compact-and-totally-disconnected","concern":"Step 1.1 invokes thm-complete-and-totally-bounded-implies-compact, whose Statement explicitly assumes the Axiom of Countable Choice, but the corollary neither assumes Choice nor restricts its compactness conclusion to that foundation.","severity":"would-be-fatal"},{"id":"thm-dirichlet-series-half-plane-convergence","concern":"Step 2.1 calls s ↦ n^{-s} holomorphic without a cited or supplied derivation that the complex exponential is holomorphic and that its composition with -s log n is holomorphic.","severity":"gap-a-reader-closes"},{"id":"thm-dirichlet-series-absolute-half-plane-holomorphy","concern":"Step 3.1 treats the displayed series as derivatives of the partial sums without establishing that (n^{-s})'=-(log n)n^{-s}; this is not among its listed facts.","severity":"gap-a-reader-closes"},{"id":"thm-dirichlet-series-abscissa-gap","concern":"Step 1.2 concludes that C n^{-1-delta} is summable but cites no p-series or comparison result; the cited half-plane theorem does not provide this estimate.","severity":"gap-a-reader-closes"},{"id":"def-generalized-nice-finite-family","concern":"Alternatives 1 and 3 use complete/anticomplete pairs and complete/anticomplete blockades without defining those terms or citing def-edges-between-sets-and-pure-mixed-pairs. The cited blockade definition supplies only ordinary blockades.","severity":"would-be-fatal"},{"id":"lem-a-complete-or-weakly-sparse-blockade-can-be-thinned-to-equal-subblocks-with-directional-sparsity","concern":"Step 1.1 falsely says weak epsilon^a-sparsity survives arbitrary restriction to X_i and X_j. Edge-density bounds are not hereditary: all permitted cross-edges can lie in the chosen subsets, so the step-2.1 estimates e_G(X_i,D_p) and e_G(X_i,X_p) are unsupported.","severity":"would-be-fatal"},{"id":"lem-a-complete-or-weakly-sparse-blockade-yields-a-complete-subblockade-or-an-anticonnected-thinning","concern":"Step 2.1 invokes a spanning tree of the connected complement and leaf-pruning to a prescribed number of vertices, but its cited fact only defines anticonnected components. The omitted graph fact is standard but supplies the construction's core.","severity":"gap-a-reader-closes"},{"id":"lem-riemann-zeta-dirichlet-series-converges-locally-uniformly","concern":"Step 1.1 uses |exp(-s log n)|=exp(-Re(s) log n), but its facts only define exp by a power series and define the real logarithm. The modulus identity is not supplied.","severity":"gap-a-reader-closes"},{"id":"thm-euler-product-for-riemann-zeta","concern":"Step 1.1 similarly uses |p^{-s}|=p^{-Re(s)} without an established complex-exponential modulus identity.","severity":"gap-a-reader-closes"},{"id":"thm-theta-mellin-representation-of-completed-zeta","concern":"Step 1.1 asserts that the complex sum-integral interchange is justified after observing each absolute summand is integrable, but does not show that the sum of their absolute integrals is finite. Tonelli plus the Gamma computation and zeta(sigma) would provide the missing check.","severity":"gap-a-reader-closes"},{"id":"thm-trivial-zeros-and-critical-strip","concern":"The final sentence says “if rho is a zero then so are 1-rho and conjugate(rho).” This is false for a trivial zero: rho=-2 gives 1-rho=3, while zeta(3) is nonzero. The symmetry statement must be restricted to nontrivial zeros.","severity":"would-be-fatal"},{"id":"ex-mobius-reciprocal-zeta-coefficients","concern":"The displayed finite coefficient segment ends with O(8^{-s}), although no asymptotic variable or uniform meaning of that O-term is specified for complex s. The finite truncation itself is correct.","severity":"presentation"}],"alerts":[],"seams_checked":[{"from":"absolute-values-completions-and-p-adic-numbers","to":"pro-p-groups-and-the-p-adic-integers","faithful":true,"note":"The citing page uses the prerequisite as the compatible-residue/inverse-limit Z_p model needed for the later comparison with the metric completion. The target page supplies exactly that model and its topology; no domain, hypothesis, or conclusion is altered."}]}